`swift.py` resolved `recv.method(...)` on a typed receiver as
`f"{type_name}.{callee_name}"` in the symbol tables — by BARE method name. Swift
identifies a method by its labels, so `removeItem(at:)` and `removeItem(atPath:)`
are different methods, and a project extension declaring one captured a call to
the other: the catalogued Foundation boundary vanished behind a resolved edge to
a member the call cannot compile against. PR #757 closed the static-vs-instance
half of the same mis-binding; this is the label half.

Verified live before the edit, with a label-compatible control in the same
fixture:

    extension FileManager { func removeItem(atPath p: String) -> Bool }
    fm.removeItem(at: URL(...))   -> resolved to FileManager.removeItem   WRONG
    fm.createFile(atPath: "/x")   -> resolved to FileManager.createFile   RIGHT

THE FIRST IMPLEMENTATION WAS HALF THE SPECIFIED DESIGN AND THE MEASUREMENT SAID
SO. The item specifies "key project members by name+labels where the signature is
known, fall back to bare name only when the callee has ONE project overload". The
first attempt did only the refusal, asking about whichever symbol survived
`local_symbols[Type.method]`. Alamofire: 340 binds withdrawn, and classifying each
against what the repository actually declares put 291 of them in OVERLOAD COLLAPSE
— a compatible declaration existed, it just was not the one the by-name dict kept
(`headers.index(of:)` refused because the survivor was `index(after:)`) — against
49 genuinely false binds. Trading 291 true binds for 49 correct refusals is the
wrong direction, so that version was reverted rather than shipped.

What ships asks the question of EVERY overload. `register_symbol` is the one place
they are all still visible: the base calls it for every symbol of every file
before Pass 2, and swift's override then overwrites by `Type.method`, so by Pass 2
all but one declaration's labels are gone. Accumulating them there lets
`_swift_labels_admit_call` ask whether ANY declaration admits the call.

`Symbol.meta["arg_labels"]` records what a declaration requires — the first
`simple_identifier` of each `parameter`, with `_` recorded as `None` so an
unlabelled call compares equal — carried as data rather than re-parsed from
`signature`, whose parameter types contain commas. The map is cleared in
`post_process`, because the analyzer is a module-level singleton and the base
clears its own registries at the same point for the same reason.

The refusal stays ONE-SIDED: it fires only on a label the CALL supplies that no
declaration has. Every stricter direction loses true binds — a defaulted parameter
is called with a subset of its labels, a trailing-closure call supplies no
`value_arguments` at all, and a name with no recorded labels has nothing to
compare.

One correction to the filing, which named Alamofire's
`Tests/FileManager+AlamofireTests.swift` as the live instance: both of that file's
`removeItem` declarations are `static`, so #757 already covers them. The defect is
real and reproduces on the minimal fixture; that named instance had already been
closed by the sibling fix.

MEASURED with the shipped rule, per call site, both arms in one process, and
every changed row read back at source:

  Alamofire         10,699 sites,  6 changed
  VernissageServer  19,651 sites,  3 changed

All 9 read back CORRECT and none is a lost true bind:

  * 5 Alamofire refusals are a MIS-TYPED RECEIVER. `headers.index(of:)` and
    `headers.remove(at:)` are Array methods — `index(of:)` is declared in
    `extension [HTTPHeader]`, not in `HTTPHeaders` — and the receiver had been
    typed `HTTPHeaders`, so `HTTPHeaders.index` (which only declares
    `index(after:)`) was capturing them. `arrayEncoding.encode(_:atIndex:)` is
    the same shape against a NESTED type.
  * 3 VernissageServer refusals are one shape:
    `application.services.statusesService.get(id:on:)`, whose receiver was typed
    `Application`, had been binding to `Application.get(path:withVersion:)`.
  * 1 Alamofire change is the item's own shape, on a real site:
    `Tests/FileManager+AlamofireTests.swift:43` calls Foundation's
    `createDirectory(atPath:withIntermediateDirectories:attributes:)` from
    INSIDE a project `static func createDirectory(atPath:)`, and had been
    binding to that project member. It now emits
    `swift:FileManager:0-0:createDirectory:unresolved` — the catalogued
    boundary, recovered from the extension that was shadowing it.

io-boundaries are UNCHANGED on both (Alamofire 40 edges / 30 chains,
VernissageServer 8 / 8): the recovered `createDirectory` site joins two that the
catalogue already reached through other call sites, so the chain count does not
move.

